This code defines two methods: `selectXpath` and `sourceNodes`. The first method takes an XPath expression and a document as input, evaluates the XPath expression on the document, and returns a list of nodes that match the XPath expression. The second method takes a list of nodes and a class type as input, filters the list to include only nodes that match the specified class, and returns the filtered list.

The `Element` class is used to represent an XML element with text content. Note that this implementation is simplified and may not cover all edge cases or support all features of the DOM API.

Example usage:

```java
Document doc = new Document();
Element root = new Element("root");
doc.appendChild(root);

Element child1 = new Element("child1");
child1.setTextContent("Child 1");
root.appendChild(child1);

Element child2 = new Element("child2");
child2.setTextContent("Child 2");
root.appendChild(child2);

NodeList nodeList = doc.selectXpath("//child/*");
System.out.println(nodeList.toString());

nodeList = doc.sourceNodes(nodeList, String.class);
System.out.println(nodeList.toString());
```

This code creates a sample document with an XML structure and uses the `selectXpath` and `sourceNodes` methods to filter the nodes based on the XPath expression and class type. The resulting node lists are printed to the console.
